      "id": "district-state-holidays",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcp",
      "title": "Placeholder for the state holidays required by FRCP 6(a)(6)(C),\nFRCrimP 45(a)(6)(C), and FRBP 9006(a)(6)(C) where the state's holiday\ntext is not vendored: this calendar deliberately has no dates, so a date\nthat depends on such a holiday remains an earlier-date result (the\nmissing source is the district's state-holiday statute or rule).\n\nState holidays. Each state, the District of Columbia and each territory\nwhose holiday text is vendored is one module, lit/core/states/<state>.lit\n(`core.states.<state>`), holding only what is that state's: its calendar\n`<state>-holidays` (the days `us-federal-holidays` does not already\nclose, each in force from the date its vendored text is dated), and the\nrules that read it, `frcp6-<state>` (the 2023 FRCP 6(a) with the\ncalendar as `forward_holidays`) and `frbp9006-<state>` (the same for\nFRBP 9006(a), over that state's bankruptcy courts); Delaware adds\n`frcrimp45-delaware`. Its text is bound in lit/core/Lit.toml beside the\nothers. A district or bankruptcy package in the state imports its rule\nfrom that module, names it as its `counting` rule and `recounts` the\nnational package, so adding a state is adding its module and binding,\nnever editing this calendar or core's `frcp6` and `frbp9006`. Hawaii's\nbankruptcy court declares its own list from its local rule\n(`bankr-hi`). The states and territories whose holiday text is still not\nvendored are listed in README.md (\"Deferred: state sources\"); their\ndistricts keep this empty calendar until those sources are added. The\nTenth Circuit BAP (`bap10`) counts Colorado holidays only, because a\nmatter does not identify its originating district."
    },
